I'm afraid that the unbelievable is the truth (though I can't say how=20
large Zapf's contribution was and exactly what it consisted of). The=20
font (family) you refer to must be Computer Modern, right?

Perhaps Zapf's own words could help you believe:
 "My collaboration with Don Knuth started in 1979, when he was working=20
on his Computer Modern design. An extended correspondence preceded our=20
first meeting. In 1980, I was invited to Stanford University, to work=20
within the Computer Modern project."
 "In 1980, with Stanford University students [...] we employed METAFONT
to develop Knuth?s conception of Computer Modern. I stayed at Stanford=20
for two weeks. It was extraordinary how fast Don Knuth grasped rather=20
complicated details, as if he had previously worked on the design of=20
letterforms."
 "Back to Computer Modern. I regarded this type as a little too thin for=20
a text font, but we could easily produce a stronger version from the=20
METAFONT data later on. In particular, I foresaw problems with greatly=20
reduced documents. As soon as we had digitized a structure of the type=20
everything went rapidly."
...all from an English translation of Zapf's article "Meine=20
Zusammenarbeit mit Don Knuth und meine Schriftentw=FCrfe" from January=20
2000, available at <http://www.tug.org/TUGboat/Articles/tb22-1-
2/tb70zapf.pdf>.

Later they went on to create AMS Euler together - which seems much more=20
Zapf's work.

